{{/*
  The default templates are meant to be readable, correct, and replaced. Point
  TEMPLATE_DIR at a directory holding a file of the same name *in the same
  subdirectory* to override any of them, and STATIC_DIR at one holding your own
  styles.css or logo.svg — neither needs a rebuild.

  Class names are the contract between these templates and styles.css. Overriding one
  and not the other works as long as the names hold, which is why they describe what
  a thing *is* — .site-header, .account-menu — rather than how it looks.
*/}}

{{/*
  Everything from the doctype to </head>, shared by both layouts. It lives in a
  partial rather than being written twice because the two settings below are
  load-bearing and a second copy is a second place to get them wrong.
*/}}
{{define "document_head"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}} — {{.SiteName}}</title>
{{/*
  Two settings, both load-bearing. Keep them if you override "document_head".

  includeIndicatorStyles:false — htmx injects a <style> block for the indicator
  classes as soon as it loads, and style-src 'self' blocks it. Silently, apart from
  a console warning, and it fails *open*: the rule that hides an hx-indicator never
  applies, leaving a spinner permanently visible. So the injection is off and
  styles.css carries those rules instead. Keep the .htmx-indicator rules if you
  replace styles.css — the two halves go together.

  responseHandling — htmx's default is to swap 2xx and to swap *nothing* on 4xx or
  5xx. That default silently throws away every refusal the server sends as a 4xx
  carrying the fragment meant for the page: the browser drops it, and the person
  who clicked watches nothing happen. Errors are swapped here instead, and error:true is kept so
  htmx:responseError still fires for anything listening.

  The other half of that rule lives in the server: a response that is a whole page
  rather than a fragment sends HX-Retarget: body, so an error page replaces the
  document instead of being pasted into whatever small element asked. See
  internal/handler/errors.go.
*/}}
<meta name="htmx-config" content='{"includeIndicatorStyles":false,
  "responseHandling":[{"code":"204","swap":false},
                      {"code":"[23]..","swap":true},
                      {"code":"[45]..","swap":true,"error":true}]}'>
{{/*
  Both bundled, both served from this origin: no CDN, so the CSP stays 'self' for
  scripts and styles alike and no third-party server sits on the payment path.
*/}}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{asset "styles.css"}}">
{{/*
  A hosted font service's stylesheet, when FONT_CSS_URL names one — an Adobe Fonts
  kit is the usual case. It comes before nothing and after styles.css deliberately:
  the kit only declares @font-face rules, so order against the theme does not
  matter, and putting it second keeps the site's own stylesheet the one that loads
  first if the service is slow.

  FONT_ORIGINS must list this URL's origin, and the origin serving the font files —
  for a Typekit kit those are use.typekit.net and p.typekit.net, two hosts because
  the kit CSS and the .woff2 files it points at live on different ones. The server
  refuses to boot if FONT_ORIGINS omits the stylesheet's own origin, but it cannot
  know the second one, so a kit that loads and still renders in the fallback font
  means p.typekit.net is missing from the list.

  Only the <link> form of an embed is supported. A font service's JavaScript loader
  would need script-src widened and a nonce for the inline snippet it hands you;
  see internal/middleware/securityheaders.go for why that answer is no.
*/}}
{{with .FontCSSURL}}<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{.}}">{{end}}
<script src="{{asset "htmx.min.js"}}" defer></script>
</head>
{{end}}
